<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="3-methylthiopropanoate biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5389" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: CPD-7672 3-Methylthiopropanoate| is produced by several routes: (i) catabolism of methionine |CITS:[479166]|, (ii) demethylation of dimethylsulfoniopropionate in bacteria |CITS:[8899985]| or (iii) a shunt route of the |FRAME:PWY-5389|. In the latter route, represented here, 1,2-dihydroxy-3-keto-5-methylthiopentene is oxidized to formate and 3-methylthioproprionate. The purpose of this shunt route, also known as the off-pathway reaction of methionine salvage, is yet unknown; it has been postulated that it might provide a mechanism for regulating methionine levels in vivo |CITS: [11371200]|. The enzyme responsible for the formation of |FRAME:CPD-7672| is acireductone dioxygenase. This enzyme has been found in a number of species and has been shown to be involved in the catalysis of two reactions: the Fe&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;-dependent conversion of |FRAME:CPD-85| into |FRAME:CPD-479| (EC 1.13.11.53) and the Ni&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;-dependent conversion of the same substrate into |FRAME:CPD-7672| (EC 1.13.11.54) |CITS:[16989860]|. This enzyme has been characterized for |FRAME:TAX-573| (see |FRAME:MONOMER-1323|) and in rice. The latter was shown to catalyze mostly the Fe&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;-acireductone dioxygenase step. Indeed, this enzyme appears to have a preference for Fe&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt; rather than Ni&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt; suggesting it might be involved mostly in the |FRAME:PWY-4361| rather than the shunt pathway producing the cytotoxic compound 3-&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-methylthiopropionate |CITS:[16297065]|. &lt;b&gt;This Pathway in Plants:&lt;/b&gt; In plants, the production of 3-methylthiopropionate remains uncertain. The compound is cytotoxic to plant cells and has been reported in leaves of cassava infected by |FRAME:TAX-339| as a blight-inducing toxin |CITS:[perreaux86]|. It is known to be produced by phytopathogens and by soil microorganisms, and to affect the growth of plant seedlings |CITS:[12715879]|.</Description>
